<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Val d'Ossola</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Val d'Ossola is an Alpine valley system in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, drained by the river Toce and bordered on three sides by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Its main town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domodossola">Domodossola</a>, and it carries the road and railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simplon-pass">Simplon Pass</a>. In autumn 1944 Italian partisans drove out the German and Fascist garrisons and ran the short-lived Republic of Ossola.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/val-dossola.jpg" alt="Val d'Ossola" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Vecchi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valle_Ossola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Ossola lies north of Lago Maggiore and is made up of the main valley, the Ossola proper, and its side valleys, among them Anzasca, Antrona, Bognanco, Divedro, Antigorio, Formazza, Isorno and Vigezzo. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennine-alps">Pennine Alps</a> close it to the west and the Lepontine Alps to the north and east. Its highest point is the Nordend, a peak of the Monte Rosa Massif and the highest summit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>; other important mountains are the Pizzo d'Andolla, the Monte Leone and the Corno Cieco.</p><p>The name has been explained in many ways, most likely as Celtic for high lands. The valley's German name, Eschental, and its Walser name, Eschetaal, mean valley of the ash trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1944, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> held south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s Italian Social Republic in control of all Northern Italy, Italian partisans led by the Committee of National Liberation of Upper Italy rose behind the German lines. The rising produced several provisional partisan governments across Northern Italy, of which the Free Republic of Ossola was the most prominent. It was officially recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and by Allied consulates there. Within a few weeks German reinforcements crushed the uprising, and the valley was not freed until the final offensives of 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ossola belongs to the Province of Verbano-Cusio-Ossola in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domodossola">Domodossola</a> its main towns are Villadossola, Crevoladossola, Ornavasso and Mergozzo. It holds the Alpe Veglia and Alpe Devero Natural Park and the Alta Valle Antrona Natural Park, and part of the Val Grande National Park, the largest wilderness area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ossola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1944 Italian partisan formations drove German and Fascist garrisons out of the <b>Val d'Ossola</b>, near the Swiss border, and entered Domodossola on 10 September.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-ossola">Republic of Ossola</a></li></ul></section>
